🔍 4. Regular Expressions (Regex)

Regex is used to define search and validation patterns.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Import

```java id="r1"
import java.util.regex.*;
```

---

📌 Common Regex Symbols

| Symbol | Meaning         |
| ------ | --------------- |
| `.`    | Any character   |
| `*`    | Zero or more    |
| `+`    | One or more     |
| `?`    | Zero or one     |
| `\\d`  | Digit           |
| `\\w`  | Word character  |
| `\\s`  | Whitespace      |
| `^`    | Start of string |
| `$`    | End of string   |

---

📌 Simple Validation

```java id="r2"
String email = "test@example.com";
boolean valid = email.matches("^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@(.+)$");

System.out.println(valid);
```

---

📌 Find Pattern

```java id="r3"
Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("\\d+");
Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher("Order123");

while (matcher.find()) {
    System.out.println(matcher.group());
}
```

Output:

```text id="r4"
123
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 Real-World Uses of Regex

* Email validation
* Password checking
* Extracting numbers
* Log analysis

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Forgetting to escape backslashes (`\\d`)
❌ Using `String` for repeated modifications
❌ Choosing `StringBuffer` when `StringBuilder` is enough

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ `StringBuffer` is mutable and thread-safe
✔ `StringBuilder` is mutable and faster
✔ Regex validates and searches text patterns
✔ `matches()` checks complete string patterns

